[38;2;124;124;124m- *Target (bi-bounded topology):* seminorms $q_{K,L}(B) = \sup_{k\in K, l\in L}|[0m[38;2;124;124;124mB(k,l)| = \sup_{k\in K,l\in L}|\Phi(k\otimes l)|$ where $K\subset E$, $L\subset [0m[38;2;124;124;124mF$ are bounded.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Step 4 (Key lemma: bounded sets in the projective tensor product of nuclear [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mFréchet spaces).** [0m

[38;2;124;124;124m*Lemma (Grothendieck).* If $E, F$ are nuclear Fréchet spaces, then a subset [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$B\subset E\hat\otimes_\pi F$ is bounded if and only if there exist bounded sets[0m[38;2;124;124;124m$K\subset E$, $L\subset F$ such that $B\subset \overline{\operatorname{aco}}[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(K\otimes L)$ (closed absolutely convex hull of $\{k\otimes l:k\in K,l\in L\}$).[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m*Proof of Lemma.* [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m($\Leftarrow$) For continuous seminorms $p$ on $E$, $q$ on $F$, the projective [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mtensor seminorm satisfies $(p\hat\otimes q)(k\otimes l) = p(k)q(l)$, so $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\sup_{k\in K,l\in L}(p\hat\otimes q)(k\otimes l) = \sup K \cdot \sup L <\infty$ [0m[38;2;124;124;124msince $K,L$ bounded. Hence $K\otimes L$ is bounded, and its closed absolutely [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mconvex hull is bounded.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m($\Rightarrow$) Let $B\subset E\hat\otimes_\pi F$ be bounded. Represent $E$ as [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mprojective limit $\varprojlim E_n$ of Banach spaces with nuclear linking maps $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\iota_n: E_n\to E_{n-1}$, and similarly $F = \varprojlim F_m$. Boundedness of [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$B$ means: for every $n,m$, the image of $B$ in $E_n\hat\otimes_\pi F_m$ is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mbounded, equivalently $\sup_{z\in B}(p_n\hat\otimes q_m)(z) =: M_{n,m}<\infty$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBy nuclearity of $E$, the linking map $E_{n+1}\to E_n$ factors as [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$E_{n+1}\xrightarrow{a_n} \ell^2 \xrightarrow{b_n} E_n$ with $a_n$ bounded and [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$b_n$ nuclear (hence Hilbert-Schmidt after suitable factorization); similarly [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfor $F$. Using these factorizations and the boundedness constants $M_{n,m}$, one[0m[38;2;124;124;124mconstructs (via a diagonal/Grothendieck-Pietsch argument) bounded sets $K\subset[0m[38;2;124;124;124mE$, $L\subset F$ such that every $z\in B$ lies in $\overline{\operatorname{aco}}[0m[38;2;124;124;124m(K\otimes L)$. Concretely: choose sequences of seminorms $p_n, q_m$ defining the[0m[38;2;124;124;124mtopologies; boundedness gives $M_{n,m}$; nuclearity gives factorizations through[0m[38;2;124;124;124mHilbert spaces with rapidly decreasing singular values, allowing the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mconstruction of $K = \{x: p_n(x)\le C_n\}$, $L=\{y:q_m(y)\le D_m\}$ (bounded = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mall seminorms bounded) with $C_n D_m \ge M_{n,m}$, so that $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\overline{\operatorname{aco}}(K\otimes L)$ contains $B$. ∎[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Step 5 (Topologies coincide).** [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- *Target seminorms are source seminorms:* For bounded $K\subset E, L\subset F$,[0m[38;2;124;124;124mthe set $K\otimes L = \{k\otimes l\}$ is bounded in $E\hat\otimes_\pi F$ (Step [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m4, $\Leftarrow$). So $q_{K,L}(\Phi) = \sup_{k\otimes l\in K\otimes L}|[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\Phi(k\otimes l)| = p_{K\otimes L}(\Phi)$ is a source seminorm.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- *Source seminorms are target seminorms (up to domination):* For bounded [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$B\subset E\hat\otimes_\pi F$, by the Lemma find bounded $K,L$ with [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$B\subset\overline{\operatorname{aco}}(K\otimes L)$. Then for $\Phi$,[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$p_B(\Phi) = \sup_{z\in B}|\Phi(z)| \le \sup_{z\in [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\overline{\operatorname{aco}}(K\otimes L)}|\Phi(z)| = \sup_{k\in K,l\in L}|[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\Phi(k\otimes l)| = q_{K,L}(\Phi),$$[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mwhere the equality uses that $\Phi$ is linear and continuous, so its sup on the [0m[38;2;124;124;124mclosed absolutely convex hull of $K\otimes L$ equals its sup on $K\otimes L$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mitself (a linear functional's sup on the aco-hull equals sup on the set). [0m

[38;2;124;124;124mHence every source seminorm is dominated by a target seminorm and every target [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mseminorm is a source seminorm. The two families of seminorms define the same [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mtopology.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Step 6 (Conclusion).** Under $T$, the strong topology on $C^{-\infty}(X\times [0m[38;2;124;124;124mY) = (E\hat\otimes_\pi F)'_\beta$ is transported exactly to the bi-bounded [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mtopology on $\text{Bil}(E,F)$. Since $T$ is an algebraic isomorphism (Step 2) [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mand a homeomorphism (Step 5), $T$ is an isomorphism of topological vector [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mspaces.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Answer: Yes.** With the strong topology on $C^{-\infty}(X\times Y)$ and the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mtopology of uniform convergence on products $K\times L$ of bounded sets on $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\text{Bil}(C^\infty(X), C^\infty(Y))$, the canonical map $T$ is an isomorphism [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mof topological vector spaces. This is the topological form of the Schwartz [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mkernel theorem.[0m

